#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "ef6bfc82-5993-514e-bbb4-6b3769bec0e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-752" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=e^{-x}$ and $a=3.$\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ f(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-{e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(3) & =-{e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-3}+(-{e}^{-3})(x-3)+\\frac{({e}^{-3})}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(-{e}^{-3})}{3!}(x-3)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}-{e}^{-3}(x-3)+\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{2}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{6}(x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "e82fb0f9-372a-50ab-a6a5-c1dc8868a2d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-753" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9.1} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(9.1-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(9.1-9)^{2} \\\\  & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(0.1)-\\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.016\\,620,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places. \n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0038b334-e6d3-5ea6-92f7-e9cc7fc417a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0038b334-e6d3-5ea6-92f7-e9cc7fc417a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310616} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c075d1a-08c8-5a2f-9124-b9d5609b9985" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f2c13f1-81f7-545f-882e-aa6e418d6c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-347635" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{1.2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{1/2}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{(-1/4)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.2$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n1.2-1=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{1}{8}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{200} \\\\  & =\\frac{219}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a3b73c8-441e-5595-af0a-40fc3c0044a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a3b73c8-441e-5595-af0a-40fc3c0044a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310619} :answer-field/id #uuid "76e2ac35-9b2d-5677-9538-808448878cfc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b364c5d0-46c3-5c92-834b-0849c46c5e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b364c5d0-46c3-5c92-834b-0849c46c5e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310621} :answer-field/id #uuid "771391b4-e95f-5fed-9c1d-e057532cc808" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d889759e-ad5c-52fb-8505-431f6294d5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d889759e-ad5c-52fb-8505-431f6294d5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310623} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e6a6a64-1c09-5353-bd0d-458d03cc46f5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81f76b8b-d322-5853-848e-fee21cd6de31" :question/math-academy-id "q-353904" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about $x=e.$\n\n$x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-e)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-e)^{2}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ and $a=e.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ f(e) & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(e) & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =e+(2)(x-e)+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{e})}{2!}(x-e)^{2}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}{3!}(x-e)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =e+2(x-e)+\\frac{1}{2e}(x-e)^{2}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b5b4f0c-a8d4-5b33-8279-eaa99e44613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b5b4f0c-a8d4-5b33-8279-eaa99e44613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310626} :answer-field/id #uuid "f396c657-75b7-585a-b01c-dda57dfb6953" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310625} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16752fcf-1e06-52cf-b22f-480f0d871686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16752fcf-1e06-52cf-b22f-480f0d871686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310628} :answer-field/id #uuid "70011fbc-ce42-5c2c-9f80-987048adb492" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310627} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f2aa15e-2678-5509-85d1-d0e9b5d84fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f2aa15e-2678-5509-85d1-d0e9b5d84fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310630} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc5fdec-6177-53d9-87fe-40b399c35de6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ba4e3d-1327-530e-8993-de1be5288167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ba4e3d-1327-530e-8993-de1be5288167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310632} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c4be7e-dc44-57f7-90b0-9cdc7c2de488" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42f93c24-fffd-543d-8271-aa03ba162e9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353905" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ about $x=1.$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-4}}\n$(x-1)^{3}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =1+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{-1}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dcad847-55c5-54c9-978e-aab990d0c5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183846} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e420be-7458-5309-8fa6-53d19346dcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183847} {:answer/id #uuid "226bcbbc-cfb5-56a0-801f-07bdeefada77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183848} {:answer/id #uuid "343b2896-72d4-5135-9e03-fd07955dee6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183849} {:answer/id #uuid "70bd5fc4-279e-5b7a-a253-acc7b5e9554c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16439" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "343b2896-72d4-5135-9e03-fd07955dee6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183849} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc495eb-3490-5a68-b1d6-e8a03d5ba5b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183845}] :question/id #uuid "fa3dd46a-8d87-5019-a6c8-488757b3b77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-9608" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nUse the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ about $x = 9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}$. Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
